# HELPER_HOST behind Caddy's automatic HTTPS (an ACME certificate for the name, no CA sign-up). Nothing here writes a
# client IP: no access log (Caddy writes one only when a site has a `log` directive), and the loggers that print a
# request (http.log.error with its remote_ip, http.stdlib's TLS handshake lines) are left out of the default log.
# HELPER_SITE is set by the local proof, which serves plain http on a local port, and by the redeploy that moves the
# helper's name (card hosts-main-on-top), which serves both names until the old one's record is deleted:
#   HELPER_SITE="ct-api.unrealized.trade, <the old name>" apps/helper/deploy/redeploy.sh ...
# Caddy puts the value in when it adapts this file, so a list of names is one site with a certificate for each. It
# takes the value from its container's environment, set when the container is made, so a reload never changes the
# names: redeploy.sh makes caddy again when the HELPER_SITE compose resolves differs from the running caddy's, keeping
# its certificates in the caddy_data volume (compose.yaml), and reloads it otherwise.
# HELPER_HOST is a sibling of SIGN_HOST, never under it: a host under SIGN_HOST could claim its passkeys
# (scripts/ci/host-children.mjs refuses one in this tree).
# An upstream failure (a 502 or 504: the dial to the helper, its answer cut) is logged with its reason, its status and
# its time, never its request (card helper-answers-through-a-burst: the 19 Nov 14:30Z 502s left no line naming why):
# the `upstream` log takes http.log.error alone and deletes its `request` field, which holds the client's address, port
# and headers. The reason names the helper's container address, never a client's.
# Caddy keeps up to 1024 idle connections to the helper (card helper-answers-through-a-burst; Caddy's default is 32), so
# a burst's connections are reused by its next requests, never closed and dialed again: 300 page opens held 921 at
# once, and each closed one is a new connect into the helper's listen queue (src/server.ts LISTEN_BACKLOG).
# The admin endpoint is a unix socket in the caddy_config volume, reachable only from inside the container (never a TCP
# port): redeploy.sh reloads this file through it with `docker compose exec`, so a change here reaches Caddy with no gap.
# Every answer a caller asks to have compressed is sent zstd or gzip (card solana-quote-under-1s: a cold page's first
# round is ~37 KB raw, ~11 KB gzip).
{
	admin unix//config/caddy-admin.sock
	log default {
		output stderr
		exclude http.log.access http.log.error http.stdlib http.handlers
	}
	log upstream {
		output stderr
		include http.log.error
		format filter {
			request delete
		}
	}
}

{$HELPER_SITE:ct-api.unrealized.trade} {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy helper:8080 {
		transport http {
			keepalive 30s
			keepalive_idle_conns_per_host 1024
		}
	}
}
